I am working on a narrow benchmark for consequential AI-agent actions: can the workflow prove authority before execution?

Browser agents are one of the clearest cases because a credentialed submit can cross from automation into a real-world action.

The pattern I am testing is:

proposed browser action -> pre-action authority receipt -> developer-owned execute/revise/stop/human-review

For a credentialed submit, the receipt should bind at least:

- actor/runtime ref;
- target origin/page ref;
- form/action ref;
- params hash or equivalent exact-submit binding;
- authority refs;
- evidence/policy refs;
- decision route;
- trace refs;
- no downstream execution by the receipt service.

I put this into an Agent Authority Benchmark v0.1 and would value maintainer/community feedback on whether the scenario matches how browser-agent projects think about high-impact submits.

This is not a request for endorsement or integration. I am trying to make the authority-before-action test concrete.

Reference:
https://github.com/neurarelay/relay-action-card#agent-authority-benchmark
